      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "Anecdotal remarks suggest that incisions in oral soft tissue would be less painful if performed with laser rather than with scalpel, but such an argument remains to be scientifically validated."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Twelve rats received graded incisions to lip, gingiva, mucosa, and tongue with either a pulsed Er:YAG laser (65 mJ/pulse at 10 Hz) or a stainless steel scalpel. The amplitude of the jaw-opening reflex, as measured by the digastric muscle electromyogram, was used to quantify the nociceptive response evoked by the surgical incisions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Except for lip, mean reflex amplitudes evoked by laser were significantly smaller (P < .05) than those evoked by scalpel in all other incision sites."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Er:YAG laser surgery may be less painful and therefore require less use of anesthesia and sedation than conventional scalpel surgery in oral soft tissue procedures."
        }
      ]},
